    "thm-ptfa-group-rings-are-ore-domains": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-poly-torsion-free-abelian-group",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A group G is poly-torsion-free abelian (PTFA) if it admits a finite subnormal series\n1=G_0 ◁ G_1 ◁ ⋯ ◁ G_m=G\nin which every quotient G_{j+1}/G_j is torsion-free abelian. The trivial group is PTFA, using the length-zero series. The term “subnormal” means each G_j is normal in G_{j+1}; normality in all of G is not required.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-group-ring",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring ([[def-commutative-ring]]) and let $G$ be a group\n([[def-group]]). The **group ring** $R[G]$ is the free left $R$-module on the\nset $G$ ([[def-free-module-on-a-set-and-standard-basis]]).\n\nFor each $g \\in G$, write $[g] \\in R[G]$ for the standard basis vector indexed\nby $g$. Thus every element of $R[G]$ has a unique expression\n$$\\sum_{g \\in F} r_g [g]$$\nwith $F \\subseteq G$ finite and $r_g \\in R$.\n\nThe notation is deliberately that of **formal** finite sums: at this stage\n$R[G]$ is only the underlying free $R$-module with its distinguished basis. The\nmultiplication satisfying\n$$[g][h]=[gh]$$\nand the resulting $R$-algebra structure are constructed in\n[[thm-group-ring-is-a-unital-algebra-with-basis-g]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-group-ring-is-a-unital-algebra-with-basis-g",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $G$ be a group. Write $[g] \\in R[G]$ for\nthe basis vector of [[def-group-ring]] indexed by $g \\in G$.\n\nThere is a unique $R$-bilinear multiplication on $R[G]$ satisfying\n$$[g][h]=[gh]\\qquad(g,h\\in G).$$\nWith this product, $R[G]$ is a unital $R$-algebra whose underlying\n$R$-module has basis $\\{[g]:g\\in G\\}$. Its identity is $[e]$, where $e$ is the\nidentity of $G$, and every basis element $[g]$ is a unit with inverse\n$[g^{-1}]$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-torsion-free-abelian-extensions-preserve-group-ring-domains",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $k$ be a commutative field and let $H\\trianglelefteq G$ be a normal subgroup with torsion-free abelian quotient $G/H$. If $k[H]$ has no zero divisors, then $k[G]$ has no zero divisors. The group extension need not split; neither finite generation of $G/H$ nor an order on all of $G$ is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-ptfa-groups-have-finite-folner-sets",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every group admitting a finite subnormal series with torsion-free abelian factors satisfies the left Folner condition: for each finite subset $S$ and each $\\varepsilon>0$ there exists a finite nonempty subset $F$ with $|sF\\triangle F|<\\varepsilon|F|$ for every $s\\in S$. This assertion requires no countability or finite-generation hypothesis and no choice axiom. More generally the finite Folner condition is preserved by arbitrary group extensions whose kernel and quotient satisfy it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-folner-group-ring-domains-have-ore-fractions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $k$ be a commutative field and let $G$ satisfy the finite left Folner condition. If $k[G]$ has no zero divisors, then it satisfies both Ore conditions: for nonzero $a,b\\in k[G]$ there exist nonzero $x,y$ with $ax=by$, and nonzero $u,v$ with $ua=vb$. Moreover it embeds as a unital subring in a division ring $D$ in which each element has the form $as^{-1}$ for $a\\in k[G]$ and $0\\ne s\\in k[G]$. Thus this is a skew field of fractions, rather than only a ring in which some denominators are invertible. The construction uses no choice axiom.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix a finite series $1=G_0\\trianglelefteq G_1\\trianglelefteq\\cdots\\trianglelefteq G_m=G$ as in [F1]. Its base ring $\\mathbb Q[G_0]=\\mathbb Q$ has no zero divisors. For each $j<m$, apply [F3] to $G_j\\trianglelefteq G_{j+1}$ and its torsion-free abelian quotient. Induction gives absence of zero divisors in $\\mathbb Q[G]$. The domain supplier keeps the conjugation automorphisms and cocycle units of the extension in its leading-coefficient product, so this induction assumes neither a splitting nor normality of each $G_j$ in the final group.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "By [F4], the same finite subnormal series gives finite left Folner sets in $G$. That supplier constructs finite sets directly across each group extension, with only finitely many coset lifts for each test, rather than assuming an invariant mean or adding a choice principle.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F5] to the ring from step 1.1 and the finite sets from step 1.2. It gives nonzero common right multiples $ax=by$ of every nonzero $a,b$, and the group-inversion anti-automorphism gives nonzero common left multiples. It then constructs a division ring from equivalence classes of right-linear maps on nonzero right ideals, embeds $\\mathbb Q[G]$ injectively, and shows every element of that division ring is $as^{-1}$ with $0\\ne s\\in\\mathbb Q[G]$. These are exactly both Ore conditions and the required skew field of fractions. The length-zero case is already included by the base field.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
